<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa María del Naranco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">848</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santa María del Naranco is a building on Mount Naranco above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, built as a royal hall for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-i-of-asturias">Ramiro I of Asturias</a> and dated by an inscription of 848; it was later turned into a church. A two-storey hall with barrel vaults, external loggias and carved medallions, it is the most celebrated work of Asturian pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque architecture</a>. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> of the Monuments of Oviedo and the Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-maria-del-naranco.jpg" alt="Santa María del Naranco" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Radiostud484 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SaintMaryOfNaranco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…kingdom produced a distinctive pre-Romanesque architecture, including San Julián de los Prados in Oviedo and the hall of <b>Santa María del Naranco</b> and the church of San Miguel de Lillo built under Ramiro I, together with metalwork such as the Cross of the Angels of 808 and the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
